# Hand-rolled binary state-file writers

Notes for writing an undocumented binary state file that a desktop client
owns. The worked example is a key/value tree format (Steam's binary VDF), but
the failure modes are generic to any format assembled by hand.

## The failure mode that hides best

A helper that appends to an immutable value does not mutate it:

```python
def _w_str(buf, s):
    buf += b"\0" + s.encode() + b"\0"   # rebinds the LOCAL name only
```

Every caller's chunk is discarded, with no error. It is survivable only
because the same function usually *also* has direct `buf += ...` lines (for
fixed-width integers) that do land — so the output is a plausible-looking file
containing some fields and silently missing all the string ones.

```python
def _w_str(parts, s):
    parts.append(b"\0")
    parts.append(s.encode("utf-8") if isinstance(s, str) else s)
    parts.append(b"\0")

def serialise(entries):
    parts = [b"\0shortcuts\0"]
    ...
    return b"".join(parts)
```

Pass a **list**, never `bytes`, and never use `+=` on the accumulator.
The same trap exists in shell: `s="a"; f(){ s="$s$b"; }` writes nothing to the
caller. Use `printf -v`, an array, or a temp file.

## Object terminators

A key/value tree needs an explicit end-of-object marker per object. Omit the
per-entry terminator and write only the outer one, and a reader runs off the
end of entry N into entry N+1's header — producing a phantom extra entry,
which reads as a parser bug rather than a writer bug. Check the entry count
at several sizes (1, 2, 3, 5); a phantom appears only once there is a
following entry to run into.

## NUL-prefixed values on read

A value written as `\0<data>\0` must have its leading NUL skipped before
scanning for the terminator. Read without skipping and the scan finds the
terminator immediately, so every string field round-trips as `""` while the
file is perfectly correct:

```python
if buf[i] != 0x00:
    break
i += 1
v, i = _rd_bytes(buf, i)
```

## Test shape

Assert on **values**, never only on counts — a count assertion passes while
every string field is empty. Cover:

| Case | Catches |
|---|---|
| single entry, all fields asserted | dropped appends, leading-NUL bug |
| N entries for N in 1,2,3,5 | missing per-object terminator |
| empty string value | falsy-vs-absent confusion |
| empty collection (zero-count + terminator) | unterminated count |
| multi-byte / unicode string and tag | char-vs-byte length assumptions |
| literal expected bytes for one entry | field order and width |
| garbage input rejected | a parser that accepts anything |

Round-trip proves the reader and writer agree **with each other**. It does not
prove the application accepts the bytes. Without a file the app itself wrote,
name the real confirmation — the app rendering the result — rather than
letting a green suite stand in for it.
